# IMO 1965, Problem 1

**Problem.** Determine all $x \in [0, 2\pi]$ satisfying
$$2\cos x \;\le\; \bigl|\sqrt{1+\sin 2x}-\sqrt{1-\sin 2x}\bigr| \;\le\; \sqrt{2}.$$

## Setup

Since $\sin 2x \in [-1,1]$, both $1+\sin 2x$ and $1-\sin 2x$ are nonnegative, so the square roots are real. Set
$$A = \sqrt{1+\sin 2x},\qquad B = \sqrt{1-\sin 2x}.$$
Then
$$|A-B|^2 = A^2 + B^2 - 2AB = 2 - 2\sqrt{(1+\sin 2x)(1-\sin 2x)} = 2 - 2\sqrt{1-\sin^2 2x} = 2 - 2|\cos 2x|.$$
Hence
$$\bigl|\sqrt{1+\sin 2x}-\sqrt{1-\sin 2x}\bigr| = \sqrt{2 - 2|\cos 2x|}.$$

## The right inequality always holds

$|\cos 2x| \ge 0$ gives $2 - 2|\cos 2x| \le 2$, so $\sqrt{2-2|\cos 2x|} \le \sqrt{2}$. The right inequality is satisfied for **every** $x$.

## The left inequality

We need $2\cos x \le \sqrt{2 - 2|\cos 2x|}$.

**Case 1: $\cos x < 0$.** Then $2\cos x < 0 \le \sqrt{2-2|\cos 2x|}$, so the inequality holds automatically.

**Case 2: $\cos x \ge 0$.** Both sides are nonnegative, so squaring preserves the inequality:
$$4\cos^2 x \le 2 - 2|\cos 2x| \iff 2\cos^2 x \le 1 - |\cos 2x| \iff |\cos 2x| \le 1 - 2\cos^2 x.$$
Using $2\cos^2 x - 1 = \cos 2x$, the right-hand side is $-\cos 2x$, so the condition becomes
$$|\cos 2x| \le -\cos 2x.$$
Since $|\cos 2x| \ge 0$, this forces $-\cos 2x \ge 0$, i.e. $\cos 2x \le 0$; and when $\cos 2x \le 0$ we have $|\cos 2x| = -\cos 2x$, giving equality. Thus in Case 2 the condition reduces to
$$\cos 2x \le 0.$$

## Combining the cases

The solution set is
$$\{x : \cos x < 0\} \;\cup\; \{x : \cos x \ge 0 \text{ and } \cos 2x \le 0\}.$$

- $\cos x < 0$ on $(\pi/2,\, 3\pi/2)$.
- $\cos x \ge 0$ on $[0,\pi/2]\cup[3\pi/2, 2\pi]$. Within these, $\cos 2x \le 0$ means $2x \in [\pi/2, 3\pi/2]\cup[5\pi/2, 7\pi/2]$, i.e. $x \in [\pi/4, 3\pi/4]\cup[5\pi/4, 7\pi/4]$. Intersecting:
  - $[\pi/4, 3\pi/4] \cap \bigl([0,\pi/2]\cup[3\pi/2,2\pi]\bigr) = [\pi/4, \pi/2]$,
  - $[5\pi/4, 7\pi/4] \cap \bigl([0,\pi/2]\cup[3\pi/2,2\pi]\bigr) = [3\pi/2, 7\pi/4]$.

So Case 2 contributes $[\pi/4, \pi/2]\cup[3\pi/2, 7\pi/4]$.

## Boundary check

At $x = \pi/2$: $\cos x = 0$, $\sin 2x = 0$, so the middle term is $\sqrt{2-2|\cos\pi|} = 0$; inequality $0 \le 0 \le \sqrt{2$ holds. ✓
At $x = 3\pi/2$: $\cos x = 0$, $\sin 2x = 0$, middle term $= 0$; holds. ✓

Thus the two cases merge into the single interval
$$[\pi/4,\, \pi/2]\;\cup\;(\pi/2,\,3\pi/2)\;\cup\;[3\pi/2,\,7\pi/4] \;=\; \left[\frac{\pi}{4},\,\frac{7\pi}{4}\right].$$

## Verification of endpoints

- $x = \pi/4$: $\cos x = \tfrac{\sqrt2}{2}$, so $2\cos x = \sqrt2$; $\sin 2x = 1$, $\cos 2x = 0$, middle term $= \sqrt{2}$. Get $\sqrt2 \le \sqrt2 \le \sqrt2$. ✓
- $x = 7\pi/4$: $\cos x = \tfrac{\sqrt2}{2}$, so $2\cos x = \sqrt2$; $\sin 2x = -1$, $\cos 2x = 0$, middle term $= \sqrt{2}$. Get $\sqrt2 \le \sqrt2 \le \sqrt2$. ✓
- $x = 0$ (outside): $2\cos x = 2$, middle term $= \sqrt{2-2\cdot1} = 0$; $2 \le 0$ fails. ✓ excluded.

## Answer

The set of all $x \in [0, 2\pi]$ satisfying the inequality is

$$\boxed{\dfrac{\pi}{4} \le x \le \dfrac{7\pi}{4}}.$$

